## What this licence covers

The measurement layer: the pipeline and eval harness (`crates/`), the
task packs and their prompts, schemas and report templates (`packs/`),
the committed baselines (`evals/`), the assurance-claims registry and
its validator (`assurance/`), and `privacy-boundary.toml`.

The desktop application (`app/`) is not published under this licence
and is not included in this repository.


## Measurement artefacts

The baselines, fixtures and claims registry are records of what was
measured on a date, not software. They are published under the same
Apache-2.0 terms for simplicity, and attribution is what is actually
asked for: if you cite a number, cite the baseline file and its
`scoring_version`, because the same run scored under a different
version is a different number.

Every fixture in this repository is invented. No row, amount, date or
document is traceable to a person; see the data rules in CLAUDE.md.
Merchant names may be real public trading names, which disclose nothing
about anyone and are used as descriptor text only — no logos, no marks,
and no suggestion of endorsement.


## Reproduction

Reproducing a measurement needs the named public weights and the
vendored llama.cpp sidecar, neither of which ships with this
repository. The honest wording is "inspectable, and re-runnable given
the weights".


## Third-party components

This software depends on open-source Rust crates, all under permissive
licences (predominantly MIT, Apache-2.0, or a dual MIT/Apache-2.0
choice). Their terms travel with them in the Cargo registry; run
`cargo metadata` for the current set.

Three dependencies are under the Mozilla Public License 2.0 —
`cssparser`, `cssparser-macros` and `dtoa-short`, reached through
`runner`'s CSS handling. MPL-2.0 is file-level copyleft and imposes
obligations only on modifications to those files, which this project
does not make. It is compatible with distribution under Apache-2.0.

`r-efi` offers `MIT OR Apache-2.0 OR LGPL-2.1-or-later`; this project
takes it under Apache-2.0.

llama.cpp (`llama-server`) is vendored as a pinned binary at build
time under its own MIT licence and is never committed to this
repository. See `sidecars/README.md`.
